==Latin==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|la|jam}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|ine-pro|*Hyā́m}}, acc.sg.f. of {{m|ine-pro|*Hyós||who, which}}. Cognate with Ancient Greek {{m|grc|ὅς}}, Sanskrit {{m|sa|यद्|tr=yás, yā, yad}}, Avestan {{m|ae|𐬫𐬋|tr=yō}}, Phrygian {{m|xpg|ιος|sc=polytonic|tr=yos}}, {{cog|got|𐌾𐌰}}, {{m|got|𐌾𐌰𐌹||yes}}, {{cog|goh|ja|ja, jā|yes}} ({{cog|de|ja}}), {{cog|ang|ġēa||yea, yes}} ({{cog|en|yea}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|jam}}
* {{la-IPA|i.am}}
* {{audio|la-cls-iam.ogg|Audio (Classical)|lang=la}}

===Adverb===
{{la-adv|iam|-}}

# [[already]]
#: {{ux|la|Birota '''iam''' refecta est.|t=The bicycle has '''already''' been repaired.}}
# [[now]]
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Curculio|5.3.5|quote=CAPPADOX: '''Iam iam''' faciam ut iusseris.<ref>[http://www.thelatinlibrary.com/plautus/curculio.shtml Latin]</ref>|trans=CAPPADOX: '''Now''', '''now''', I’ll do as you say!<ref>[http://artemis.austincollege.edu/acad/cml/rcape/comedy/5-3.html English]</ref>}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Metamorphoses|15.871|quote='''Iamque''' opus exegi, quod nec Iovis ira nec ignis nec poterit ferrum nec edax abolere vetustas.<ref>[http://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.met15.shtml Latin]</ref>|trans='''And now''', I have completed a great work, which not Jove's anger, and not fire nor steel, nor fast-consuming time can sweep away.<ref>[http://www.theoi.com/Text/OvidMetamorphoses15.html English]</ref>}}
# [[anymore]]
# [[soon]]

====Usage notes====
{{m|la|iam|Iam}} means, generally, “at some point previous” or “since some point previous”. In English, {{m|en|already}}, the most common translation, is used only to emphasize that this point might have been expected to be later, whereas {{m|en|now}} is used to emphasize that the statement was once false, even when the statement refers to a point in the past or future. {{m|la|iam|Iam}} is used to express either. (Likewise, the most common Latin word for {{m|en|now}}, {{m|la|nunc}}, denotes only the literal present moment.) Also, where {{m|la|iam}} means {{m|en|now}}, it is often used in negative sentences, in which the most common English construction uses {{m|en|anymore}}.

